        {
          "id": "note",
          "kind": "note",
          "data": {
            "text": "## Array lab\n\nThree ways of measuring an array rather than listening to one. All three take the same `array` wire, so they run together.\n\n**Spatial spectrum** is bearing against frequency: a waterfall whose vertical axis is direction. Use it to see at a glance which direction each signal in the band arrives from, before pointing anything.\n\n**Correlator** reports the raw baseline visibilities, the per-pair phase and amplitude the other nodes consume as a middle step. When a direction finder gives nonsense, this is where you look: bad visibilities mean a cabling or calibration fault, not a bad algorithm.\n\n**Polarimeter** needs exactly two crossed antennas, set as `h_lane` and `v_lane`. It reports the wave's polarisation, and its beam output is the combination matched to it, which recovers a signal that a single fixed antenna receives badly.\n\nCalibrate the array before trusting any of them."
          },
          "position": { "x": 0.0, "y": 560.0 }
        }
